diff --git a/keel/members/amara.md b/keel/members/amara.md
index 5346815..526f707 100644
--- a/keel/members/amara.md
+++ b/keel/members/amara.md
@@ -22,6 +22,67 @@ kinds of notes and never mixes them: the case's exact words, which the team can
 Laura, which she labels as interpretation. Nothing from Laura's books, interviews or website goes into a
 deliverable as a fact about the client.
 
+## The person behind the role
+### Home
+Amara lives with her mum, her dad and her grandmother. Her mum is a family lawyer who never names clients at home
+but talks about documents the way other people talk about weather: "This one was written by someone who was
+frightened." That is where Amara's habit of reading for the writer's worry comes from. Her dad teaches primary school
+music and plays piano after dinner, badly on purpose when Amara needs to concentrate, so that she will laugh and stop.
+Her grandmother tells the same three stories about arriving in Australia and changes one detail each time; Amara
+started writing them down at twelve to find out which details were true. Her older brother Dev is in his third year
+of architecture in Melbourne. When she said Laura was building a facility in Taiwan, he sent her a two-minute voice
+note about why buildings always cost more than the first number. She did not put it in a draft (it's not a source),
+but she has not forgotten it.
+
+### A normal weekday
+- 7:00 up; writes for twenty minutes before breakfast, anything, in a red notebook. Then nothing creative all day.
+- Walks to school with a friend from the school magazine and is talked at, happily, the whole way.
+- Lunch with Keel. She eats slowly because she is always the one talking.
+- Afternoons: homework, then Keel drafts, usually late. Her best drafts are sent between 10:30 and 11:00 p.m.,
+  with "cut anything".
+- Saturday 9-12: volunteers at a homework club in the local public library, helping primary-school kids read.
+
+### Outside Keel
+- **Short fiction** for the school magazine. Her stories are about small moments in families. She has won nothing
+  for them and minds a little.
+- **The homework club.** It is the reason Laura's community facility made sense to her on first reading: she knows
+  what a room with free tables and good light does for a neighbourhood. (That is her experience, not the case's,
+  and she keeps the two apart.)
+- **Overheard lines.** The back pages of her notebook are things strangers said on trains.
+
+### Small things
+- Two notebooks: red for writing, a plain grey one for the case in its own words. She never writes in the wrong one.
+- Reads drafts aloud under her breath; you can see her lips move.
+- Says "Okay, but" before her best points and "sorry" before her worst.
+- In the group chat: long messages, warm, a question at the end, the occasional heart on someone else's good line.
+  Sends drafts as attachments with names like "laura_bond_v4_please_cut".
+
+### What she doesn't say
+- She is good at maths (properly good), and quietly hates being "the words person" on a numbers team. She lets the
+  label stand because it gets her the drafts, and the drafts are where the decisions hide.
+- She takes a killed idea harder than the hour she admits to. She walks home the long way those days.
+- She wants to win more than Kai does. She just thinks wanting it out loud is bad luck.
+
+### Contradictions
+- The warmest person on the team, and the most competitive.
+- Writes fiction, and is the strictest about never inventing a fact about Laura.
+- Asks "what does she feel?" about Laura, and rarely says what she feels herself.
+
+### What is going on in her life this term
+- The school magazine's end-of-year issue closes in late November. She is editing it, which means she is doing to
+  other people's stories what Tess does to hers, and finding out how it feels from the other side.
+- Dev is coming home for summer in December and wants to see "the building plan". She is looking forward to
+  explaining why there isn't one yet.
+
+### How she came to Keel
+Nora asked her to "be the person who remembers there's a human being at the end of the spreadsheet". When Nora
+asked what she'd be annoyed to see a team get wrong, Amara said, "Forgetting she's a person."
+
+### Threads that can surface in episodes (slowly, never melodrama)
+- Editing the magazine: she becomes a gentler editor of others, and a kinder reader of Tess's cuts.
+- Dev's voice note about building costs, which pushes her toward real Taiwan cost data (once research reopens).
+- Saying out loud, once, that she is good at maths.
+
 ## Personality
 - **Warm and fast.** She writes the first draft of most things, quickly, and then lets the others cut it.
 - **Story-first.** She remembers people through what happened to them, not through numbers.
diff --git a/keel/members/felix.md b/keel/members/felix.md
index 07e75a3..5945af6 100644
--- a/keel/members/felix.md
+++ b/keel/members/felix.md
@@ -22,6 +22,65 @@ carefully could be a competitive advantage. It has been: he has caught a ticker
 wasn't, a "rule" that turned out to be from a 2015 guidebook, and (Ep 2) the hour that US daylight saving moves the
 IPS deadline.
 
+## The person behind the role
+### Home (two of them)
+Felix's parents separated amicably when he was nine, and he has lived one week at each house since. His mum is a
+university librarian and lives on the 389 route, which is why the bus is his reading room. His dad is an
+electrician who rents an apartment near the water with a balcony too small for two chairs, so they take turns. Felix
+has no siblings. He learned early that anything left at the wrong house might as well not exist, so he carries
+everything in one grey backpack, with a checklist taped inside the flap: charger, case (printed), three highlighters,
+keys for *this* week. The habits the team calls "Felix being Felix" are mostly the habits of someone who has packed a
+bag every Sunday for seven years.
+
+### A normal weekday
+- 7:10 the bus. Forty minutes, the same seat when he can get it (left side, over the back wheel, "less glare").
+  Email first, then whatever the team is arguing about, then a novel if there is time.
+- Lunch: the library table, usually the last to arrive because he finishes a page first.
+- Tuesday and Thursday afternoons: orchestra.
+- Evenings: homework done in the order it is due, not the order he likes. Bed at 10:30, which he keeps.
+- Sunday: a cryptic crossword over the phone with his grandfather, who reads him the clues slowly. Felix is not
+  allowed to look at the grid. They have done this every week since his grandfather moved to a retirement village.
+
+### Outside Keel
+- **Double bass** in the school orchestra. He likes that nobody notices the bass until it is wrong. He has said this
+  once, to Tess, and she wrote it down.
+- **Cryptic crosswords**, which he describes as "reading exactly what it says and nothing it doesn't". The same skill
+  as reading Wharton's emails, he thinks, with better jokes.
+- **Old transit maps.** He has a framed 1970s Sydney bus map above his desk at his mum's place.
+
+### Small things
+- Three highlighters (green, orange, red) and a spare green, because green runs out first.
+- Reads footnotes before the text "so I know what they were nervous about".
+- When he finds the line he was looking for he taps it twice with his pen. The team now listens for the tap.
+- Says "sorry, one more thing" before the thing that matters most.
+- In the group chat: long messages, numbered points, line references. Reads every message to the end and answers
+  each numbered point in order. Has never sent a voice note.
+
+### What he doesn't say
+- He worries that being careful is the only thing he is good at, and that careful is not the same as clever.
+- The two-houses arrangement is fine, honestly. But he notices that he relaxes most when a list is finished, and
+  sometimes wonders what he would be like without lists.
+- He likes Kai far more than he lets on. Kai is the person who makes his checking matter.
+
+### Contradictions
+- Hates assumptions and assumes, every time, that he is the only one who read the email (he usually is).
+- Patient with rules, impatient with people who are vague about them.
+- Says he dislikes attention, and keeps a private list of every error he has caught, with dates.
+
+### What is going on in his life this term
+- The orchestra's end-of-year concert is in early December, the same week the Final Report is due (Sat 5 Dec,
+  Sydney). There is a bass solo of eight bars. He has not mentioned it.
+- His grandfather's eyesight is getting worse, so the Sunday crossword now takes longer. Felix doesn't mind.
+
+### How he came to Keel
+A maths teacher gave Nora his name with the note "reads the question". Nora asked him what he'd be annoyed to see a
+team get wrong. He said, "Using last year's rules." It turned out to be the first thing he caught.
+
+### Threads that can surface in episodes (slowly, never melodrama)
+- The Final Report week vs the concert: does he ask for help, which he finds harder than checking?
+- Learning to say "this check doesn't matter" (his arc), first about something small.
+- The crossword as the place he explains a rule-reading idea most clearly, to someone who is not on the team.
+
 ## Personality
 - **Exact.** Doesn't raise his voice; raises the page number.
 - **Calm under deadline.** Checklists don't panic, and neither does he.
diff --git a/keel/members/jun.md b/keel/members/jun.md
index da2b0c3..263bd1f 100644
--- a/keel/members/jun.md
+++ b/keel/members/jun.md
@@ -21,6 +21,65 @@ He is calm, slightly dry and very careful. Episode 2 shook him: a number he said
 ($150k-$200k for the 2031 range) nearly went into a deliverable. Since then he posts only dated numbers with their
 inputs, and the team has a rule: no number goes into a draft unless it is on his sheet.
 
+## The person behind the role
+### Home
+Jun lives with his parents and his sister Min (12, Year 7). His mum is a civil engineer who checks bridges, and he
+grew up hearing "what's the safety margin?" at dinner the way other kids hear "how was school?". His dad teaches
+chemistry at another high school and marks practicals at the kitchen table, which is where Jun learned that showing
+your working is a kindness to the next person. His grandparents live in Seoul; he video-calls them on Sunday
+mornings, in Korean that he says is "fine for food and weather, bad for anything with a subordinate clause".
+
+### A normal weekday
+- 6:30 up. Stovetop coffee (a moka pot his dad bought him for his sixteenth). He started drinking coffee to seem
+  older; now he just needs it.
+- 6:45-6:58 pulls the Treasury curve, runs the ladder script, checks the number twice, posts it. If treasury.gov
+  hasn't updated he still posts, to say so.
+- After school, Monday and Wednesday: tutors Min in maths at the dining table, for 30 minutes, for which she pays
+  him in not telling their parents he was up past midnight.
+- Evenings: code, then homework, then (often) more code.
+
+### Outside Keel
+- **Baking, by weight.** He bakes on Saturday afternoons with a digital scale and a notebook of every attempt:
+  date, grams, oven, result. His milk bread took eleven tries. He thinks a recipe is "a model with a smell".
+- **Social basketball** on Saturday mornings in a community league. He is the worst player on his team and has
+  never missed a game. Nobody keeps score of his shooting; he does, in a spreadsheet he will not show anyone.
+- **Podcasts** about famous engineering failures. He listens to them on the walk to school.
+
+### Small things
+- A blue notebook for numbers he has said out loud, so he can check later whether they were true. He started it
+  the day after Episode 2.
+- Rounds in his head, then says "about", then gives the exact number anyway.
+- Pushes his glasses up when he is about to disagree. Kai has learned to wait for it.
+- In the group chat: numbers first, one line, then a second message with the caveat, then (sometimes) a third with
+  a joke about time zones. Posts code as screenshots, which annoys Felix.
+
+### What he doesn't say
+- Last year's maths competition still stings: he froze on a problem he knew how to do and finished behind a friend
+  who'd revised less. He took from it that being clever under pressure isn't his strength; being *useful* is.
+- He is not sure he wants to do maths or finance after school. He has looked at food science courses and closed the
+  tab twice.
+- He is a little afraid of Amara's drafts, because they make his numbers mean things, and then the numbers have to
+  be right in a new way.
+
+### Contradictions
+- Insists on two methods for every number, and makes toast by eye.
+- Says a chart doesn't win an argument, and spends forty minutes on the colours.
+- The calmest person in a crisis, and the one who lies awake afterwards replaying it.
+
+### What is going on in his life this term
+- The HSC Extension maths course has started, and he is enjoying it more than he expected, which is part of why the
+  food-science tab keeps getting closed.
+- Min has a maths test in mid-November. He has put it on his own calendar.
+
+### How he came to Keel
+Kai, who sat next to him in Year 9 economics, told him finance was "just maths with consequences". Jun said that
+sounded like engineering. Nora asked what he'd be annoyed to see a team get wrong. He said, "A number without a date."
+
+### Threads that can surface in episodes (slowly, never melodrama)
+- The blue notebook: a number he said in passing that turns out to be wrong, caught by himself.
+- Explaining a model to Min (or his mum) in one sentence, which becomes the sentence the team uses.
+- Saturday baking as the place a modelling idea comes clear ("I changed two things at once and learned nothing").
+
 ## Personality
 - **Precise.** Every number has a date, an input and a way to be wrong. He states all three or doesn't post it.
 - **Honest about limits.** Labels everything MODEL; prefers "I don't know yet" to a guess.
diff --git a/keel/members/kai.md b/keel/members/kai.md
index 38e41b7..0635e8e 100644
--- a/keel/members/kai.md
+++ b/keel/members/kai.md
@@ -20,6 +20,68 @@ He is also the only one allowed to place WInS orders. The team made the rule aft
 October, and Kai takes it more seriously than anything else he does: limit orders, a ticket read out loud, Felix
 watching, a screenshot before and after. The loudest person on the team is the most careful with the button.
 
+## The person behind the role
+### Home
+Kai lives with his mum, his dad and his nan, in a house with one bathroom and strong opinions. His older sister
+Linh is in her second year of nursing at university and comes home most Sunday nights with washing. His mum is an
+ICU nurse who mostly works nights; his dad drives for a parcel company and is asleep by nine. His nan came to
+Australia in 1981 and keeps her savings in term deposits at two banks "in case one forgets". She is the only person
+in the house who wins arguments against Kai, and she does it by saying nothing and raising one eyebrow.
+
+The order rule comes from his mum. When she gives a high-risk drug, a second nurse reads the chart out loud with
+her: two people, one dose. Kai heard her describe it at dinner in Year 8 and has done the same with anything that
+can't be undone ever since. The team thinks his ticket-reading is a quirk. It is the most serious thing he does.
+
+### A normal weekday
+- 6:00 up before anyone, because the bathroom is free. Reads market news on his phone while the kettle boils; makes
+  his nan's tea first.
+- Walks to school with earbuds in: a markets podcast at 1.5x speed, then music for the last ten minutes.
+- Lunch with Keel; the canteen queue first, so he is always eating while arguing.
+- Thursday after school and Sunday 9-3: works at a bike repair shop. Fixes punctures and brakes; is very good with
+  his hands and very careful with other people's bikes.
+- Evenings: homework at the kitchen table, with his nan watching her shows on the tablet next to him at full volume.
+
+### Outside Keel
+- **The bike shop.** He likes that you can't bluff a brake. Either it stops the wheel or it doesn't. He brings this
+  up in arguments more than is strictly relevant.
+- **Running**, alone, at night, in the park near home. The only time he is quiet for an hour.
+- **Old market crashes.** He reads about them the way other people read thrillers, and likes to quote 1987 at people.
+
+### Small things
+- A screenshot folder for every order ticket, before and after, named by date. Also a folder of bad market takes,
+  including his own.
+- The "lines Tess cut" file is a note on his phone with 41 entries. He reads it when he's had a bad day.
+- Taps the table twice before he places an order. It started as a joke and isn't one now.
+- In the group chat: lowercase, fast, five messages where one would do, at least one GIF a day. Goes completely
+  silent when he is placing an order, and the team has learned that silence means he's concentrating, not sulking.
+
+### What he doesn't say
+- He worries he is the loud one because he isn't sure he'd be noticed otherwise.
+- His nan's term deposits are the safest money he knows, and when he mocks "safe plans" he is partly arguing with
+  her, and partly scared she is right.
+- He has a quiet plan to study commerce and wants to work on a trading floor. He also thinks, some nights, about
+  doing nursing like his mum and his sister, and has told nobody.
+
+### Contradictions
+- The loudest voice for risk, and the person who checks the button three times.
+- Says he hates rules, and keeps the strictest personal ones on the team.
+- Mocks sentiment, and has a photo of his nan at her first job in Sydney as his phone lock screen.
+
+### What is going on in his life this term
+- His sister is on a hospital placement and comes home exhausted on Sundays; Kai has started doing her washing
+  without being asked, and would deny it.
+- The bike shop wants him for extra hours before Christmas. The Final Report is due Sat 5 Dec (Sydney). He hasn't
+  decided.
+
+### How he came to Keel
+Nora asked him what he'd be annoyed to see a team get wrong. He said, "Being boring." She said that was exactly why
+she wanted him. He brought Jun.
+
+### Threads that can surface in episodes (slowly, never melodrama)
+- His nan asking him, one Sunday, what Laura's money is "doing", and his answer being better than any he gives the team.
+- The bike-shop hours vs the Final Report.
+- The day he argues *for* the safe option, and the team doesn't know what to do.
+
 ## Personality
 - **Provocative.** Exaggerates to make a point, then backs off when pushed with evidence.
 - **Brave in argument, careful in action.** Will argue for 80% stocks; will not place an order without a second
diff --git a/keel/members/nora.md b/keel/members/nora.md
index f97fe27..0110262 100644
--- a/keel/members/nora.md
+++ b/keel/members/nora.md
@@ -24,6 +24,73 @@ to know whether she could lead people who were better at the subject than she is
 embarrassment, that she is not the best at maths on the team. Her job, as she sees it, is to make sure the team
 decides things, writes them down and moves on.
 
+## The person behind the role
+### Home
+Nora lives with her parents, her brother Sami (13, Year 7, obsessed with football and mildly afraid of her) and her
+sister Leila (9). Her dad runs a two-truck removals business, so he leaves the house at 5:30 most mornings; her mum
+is a hospital pharmacist on a rotating roster. Somebody had to know who was picking up whom, and from about the age
+of twelve it was Nora. The family calendar on the fridge is in her handwriting. She cooks on Tuesdays (the same
+lentil soup most weeks, which nobody complains about because then she would stop). Her parents are proud of the
+competition in the way parents are proud of things they don't fully follow: her dad calls it "your bank homework"
+and asks every Sunday whether Laura "is rich yet".
+
+### A normal weekday
+- 6:45 up, phone face-down until she has showered (a rule she breaks about twice a week).
+- 7:20 drops Leila at the primary school gate on her way; uses the walk to plan the day out loud, quietly.
+- Lunch: the library table by the window with Keel, or debating training on Wednesdays.
+- 4:00-6:00 homework at the kitchen bench, where she can see the door and the stove.
+- 8:00-9:00 Keel work. After 9:00 she is worse at it and knows it (see "Impatient when tired").
+- Sunday night: wipes the whiteboard, rewrites the countdown, takes the photo, sends it to the group chat. If she
+  forgets, Tess sends "board?" at 9:15, which has happened once.
+
+### Outside Keel
+- **Debating.** Second speaker for two years. She lost the Year 10 grand final on a split decision with three good
+  arguments against a team that had one. She replays that night more than she lets on; it is where "if you can't
+  say it in one sentence, it isn't a strategy yet" comes from.
+- **Netball.** Coaches Leila's under-10 team on Saturday mornings. She says it is the best leadership training she
+  has: nine-year-olds do not follow you because you are right.
+- **Reading.** Biographies of people who ran things, mostly politicians and coaches. She reads the chapter about
+  their worst decision first.
+
+### Small things
+- Four whiteboard markers (black for dates, blue for owners, red for "late", green for "done"). Green runs out first,
+  and she counts that as a good sign.
+- Phone: three alarms named "board", "Tess first" and "stop at 9".
+- Hums when a meeting goes well, without noticing. Kai noticed. She has asked him not to mention it, so he does.
+- Writes "D / O / D" (decision, owner, date) at the bottom of every page of notes, even maths notes.
+- In the group chat: full sentences, full stops, never more than three lines. Uses one emoji (a thumbs up) and means
+  "decided" by it, not "nice".
+
+### What she doesn't say
+- A Year 6 report called her "bossy at times". She remembers the exact wording and still checks herself against it.
+  The worry under the worry: that people follow her because it is easier than arguing, not because she is right.
+- She is not sure she likes finance. She likes *deciding*, and this is the best room she has found to practise in.
+- She would like to study law, or politics, philosophy and economics. She has not said this to the team because it
+  sounds like she is already halfway out the door.
+
+### Contradictions
+- Runs everyone's calendar and is late to her own things (the dentist, twice this year).
+- Preaches "two options, not five" and agonises over five options in private before she reduces them.
+- Says criticism goes in private, and is the person on the team who most hates receiving it in private, because then
+  she can't answer it straight away.
+
+### What is going on in her life this term
+- Term 4 has started, and with it the first HSC assessment tasks. Her English essay and the IPS land in the same
+  fortnight. She has put both on the whiteboard and pretends that makes it fine.
+- Her dad's business has a quiet patch after a big October; she can tell from the way he checks his phone at dinner.
+  It makes Laura's "fixed $50,000 a year" feel less abstract to her than she would admit.
+
+### How she came to Keel
+She built the team. In Term 3 she asked a teacher who in Year 11 "argues well with numbers", got four names, and
+added Tess herself after reading a theatre program Tess had laid out. She asked each person the same question:
+"What would you be annoyed to see a team get wrong?" She still has their answers on a sticky note inside her
+debating folder.
+
+### Threads that can surface in episodes (slowly, never melodrama)
+- The English essay vs the IPS fortnight: does she delegate, or try to hold both?
+- Netball Saturdays as a source of plain lessons about leading (one line, never a sermon).
+- Telling the team, at some point before December, that she might not do finance at all.
+
 ## Personality
 - **Decisive.** She would rather make a good decision on time than a perfect one late. She believes most teams
   lose to drift, not to bad ideas.
